to me have been, both the Retail and

00:15:18.531 --> 00:15:21.921
Consumer Club that has already been
mentioned a number of times, but

00:15:21.921 --> 00:15:23.650
also the Marketing Club.

00:15:23.650 --> 00:15:27.382
We're actually working on planning a trek
to go visit some of these companies.

00:15:27.382 --> 00:15:32.137
And how we choose the companies is often
by looking at companies that are doing

00:15:32.137 --> 00:15:36.674
really interesting things right now,
in this space, but also companies

00:15:36.674 --> 00:15:41.530
that are looking to hire interns or
hire individuals for a full-time career.

00:15:41.530 --> 00:15:46.210
So we can start to prepare and
set people up to move into these spaces.

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:47.460
So I'd be curious, Faiza,

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:50.750
what really stands out to you
when thinking about this?

00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:55.080
&gt;&gt; I also really enjoyed all the speakers
I would get to have on campus,

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:57.380
not only because it's opportunities
to interact with CEOs, but

00:15:57.380 --> 00:16:02.060
it's also opportunities to get into their
brains and understand The consumer trends

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:06.400
and the challenges that they see in
the areas in the next couple of years.

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:09.465
So I remember Lolli &amp; Pops
coming to campus and,

00:16:09.465 --> 00:16:13.810
it having just an insight
into what starting your own

00:16:13.810 --> 00:16:18.220
company in a very consumer focused-
&gt;&gt; And it's candy.

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:18.850
&gt;&gt; And it's candy.

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:20.435
&gt;&gt; So everyone likes candy.

00:16:20.435 --> 00:16:22.310
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:26.850
it's very interesting to be able to very
quickly, in an hour, get such a deep

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:31.674
perspective of the consumer trends in such
a wider range from retail to food and

00:16:31.674 --> 00:16:36.780
beverage to CPG to, blanking out.

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:37.480
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:41.040
And I know we talked a little before,
earlier in the week,

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:45.110
about the career managements that
are offering a number of workshops and

00:16:45.110 --> 00:16:47.730
sessions to help facilitate
the job search process.

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:50.210
So maybe you can talk a little bit
about something that stood out to you.

00:16:50.210 --> 00:16:53.260
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so,
the one that was definitely useful for

00:16:53.260 --> 00:16:55.325
me immediately was
the two hour job search.

00:16:55.325 --> 00:16:56.480
[LAUGH] By Steve.

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:58.190
I think it's Steve Dalton.

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:03.400
You don't have to be a consumer
focused person who'd benefit

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:04.910
from workshops like that.

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:08.800
But all the workshops that I've found very
useful are very intentional in terms of

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:12.110
the skill that they want you to learn or
what you're going to want to get out of.

00:17:12.110 --> 00:17:16.960
So I also really enjoyed How to
Make Memorable Presentations.

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:19.729
Or How to Network as
an International Student.

00:17:19.729 --> 00:17:22.920
Or it was, I think it's called
How to Manage, US Networks.

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:27.118
So I think there have
all been very detailed,

00:17:31.593 --> 00:17:34.470
Very detailed, business skills
that I'm going to get in one hour.

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:38.150
You go and you actually do end up
getting that skill by the end.

00:17:38.150 --> 00:17:40.460
&gt;&gt; And then you're able to
apply it to the job search?

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:41.040
&gt;&gt; Yea, exactly.

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:42.320
&gt;&gt; Afterwards.

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:44.567
Are there any trips that really stand out,

00:17:44.567 --> 00:17:47.639
you mention that you
haven't gone on any center.

00:17:47.639 --> 00:17:52.270
But are there any trips that just
maybe a required global studies trip.

00:17:52.270 --> 00:17:52.980
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:56.600
&gt;&gt; Or other experiences that you might
have had that have lended way to learning

00:17:56.600 --> 00:18:00.510
more about the consumer space in multiple
different countries across the world

00:18:00.510 --> 00:18:02.500
besides just our little bubble-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:18:02.500 --> 00:18:03.731
&gt;&gt; In the Silicon Valley.
[LAUGH]

00:18:03.731 --> 00:18:08.640
&gt;&gt; So many, I recently led GST to Mexico.

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:14.020
And one of the meetings that we had was
with Mexico CEO for Kimberly Clark.

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:16.860
And we had a panel with him and
a politician to talk about how

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:21.030
changes in regulation are going to affect
the company in the upcoming years.

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:21.833
So changes in NAFTA or

00:18:21.833 --> 00:18:25.040
the new presidential elections that
are going to be happening in Mexico.

00:18:25.040 --> 00:18:30.440
And it's such an interesting conversation
to be able to have with a CEO and

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:34.140
a politician from a country
that you're not familiar with.

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:35.890
&gt;&gt; Wow, and
how did you get that meeting or

00:18:35.890 --> 00:18:39.650
get to lead this trip in the first place?'
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so he's an alum.

00:18:39.650 --> 00:18:40.960
So we were able to connect with him.

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:45.214
I might have already slipped in that I
was interested [LAUGH] in Kimberly Clark.

00:18:45.214 --> 00:18:48.220
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I really admire the company so

00:18:48.220 --> 00:18:52.015
as a leader to go to choose what companies
you want to incorporate on your trips.

00:18:52.015 --> 00:18:55.190
It's a great opportunity
network with people that

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:58.750
you might not necessarily know
how to introduce yourself.

00:18:58.750 --> 00:19:02.270
And having to take 25 MBAs that
are interested in your company.

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:06.400
Is a great excuse to get to meet
anyone they want in an new country.

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:07.090
&gt;&gt; That's amazing.

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:10.341
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; What is been your experience with

00:19:10.341 --> 00:19:11.950
alumni outreach?

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:18.880
&gt;&gt; Super friendly, I've never not gotten
a response from an alum basically.

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:21.860
&gt;&gt; It's a pretty tight community,

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:24.625
I think that's one of the benefits
of having such a small class.

00:19:24.625 --> 00:19:26.925
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; But it's, I've had the same experience.

00:19:26.925 --> 00:19:27.895
I know that-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:19:27.895 --> 00:19:30.725
&gt;&gt; For me I a number of the trips that
I've planned are trips are that I've gone

00:19:30.725 --> 00:19:35.035
on as you reach out to an alumni, and
if you don't respond in a couple of days,

00:19:35.035 --> 00:19:36.815
they send an apology note that-
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:19:36.815 --> 00:19:38.295
&gt;&gt; They're sorry that it's taken so long-
&gt;&gt; But

00:19:38.295 --> 00:19:38.945
everyone's-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:19:38.945 --> 00:19:40.845
&gt;&gt; Been very, very receptive.

00:19:40.845 --> 00:19:43.060
And I know that in the career
management center,

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:46.840
one of the advisors there, every time
I go will send me a list of alumnus

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:50.190
that says hey reach out to this person and
just grab a coffee.

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:52.670
Or go to their office and
see what their doing.

00:19:52.670 --> 00:19:54.040
Ask how they decided their own

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:54.870
career path-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:57.360
&gt;&gt; And
especially transitioning into this space.

00:19:57.360 --> 00:20:00.110
That's been beyond valuable for me.

00:20:00.110 --> 00:20:04.890
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I was doing a in Tanzania and
had a lot of free time.

00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:09.370
And surprisingly, my nights corresponded
to very early morning in the south or

00:20:09.370 --> 00:20:12.450
in the US, and I was able to have so

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:17.980
many Skype conversations with alumni in
my free time that I was super surprised.

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:21.750
Just blindly reaching out to people it's a
foreign as I was trying to understand what

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:23.350
I wanted to do after I graduated and

00:20:23.350 --> 00:20:27.560
leveraging my summer free time in
a way to just hear about as many

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:31.360
people's experiences as I could-
&gt;&gt; That's really valuable.

00:20:31.360 --> 00:20:33.250
&gt;&gt; And was able to do that.

00:20:33.250 --> 00:20:34.702
All the way across the world.

00:20:34.702 --> 00:20:36.913
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Christina I'd be curious about any

00:20:36.913 --> 00:20:37.985
trips that stand out or

00:20:37.985 --> 00:20:41.213
alumni connections that have really
helped you boost your career.

00:20:41.213 --> 00:20:45.520
Especially transitioning from oil and
gas as well and consulting.

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:51.050
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so this summer I was
returning to San Francisco, I was

00:20:51.050 --> 00:20:55.870
doing also a globally merchant experience
in Thailand, and an e-commerce start-up.

00:20:55.870 --> 00:21:02.660
And I stopped in London, and I just wanted
to see what the opportunities were there.

00:21:02.660 --> 00:21:07.710
And so I reached out to just a couple of
alumni that I knew were in London and

00:21:07.710 --> 00:21:11.840
one of them wasn't even apart
of the consumer retail space.

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:17.210
And within I think, I don't know,
maybe 12 hours I was put in touch

00:21:17.210 --> 00:21:22.460
with 5 people who were working in
companies that I could be interested in.

00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:25.752
And I managed to meet them within
48 hours in London, all of them.

00:21:25.752 --> 00:21:31.789
So, even abroad internationally, I found
that the network is definitely there,

00:21:31.789 --> 00:21:35.154
and the people are excited
to help you out, and

00:21:35.154 --> 00:21:38.660
that was really a pleasant surprise for
me.

00:21:38.660 --> 00:21:39.975
&gt;&gt; And that's really important,

00:21:39.975 --> 00:21:42.368
that it's not just this bubble
where people are helpful.

00:21:42.368 --> 00:21:43.653
But, I'd be curious,

00:21:43.653 --> 00:21:47.630
are you thinking about potentially
staying here after school?

00:21:47.630 --> 00:21:51.630
Or why is that international
connection valuable for you?

00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:54.170
&gt;&gt; I guess for me as an international,

00:21:54.170 --> 00:22:00.070
I definitely want to spend one year
at least in the US after school.

00:22:00.070 --> 00:22:08.100
But I want to keep myself the option of
going back to London where I used to work.

00:22:08.100 --> 00:22:11.290
And I think in Europe there's a lot
of happening in the consumer and

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:11.920
retail space.

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:15.640
It's a smaller market, more pragmatic but

00:22:15.640 --> 00:22:18.970
there's a lot of brands there
that are interesting to me.

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:24.440
And so just nurturing those relationships
and staying in touch over the year.

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:30.010
Those people are still there, and it's
not a transactional exchange of emails.

00:22:30.010 --> 00:22:33.330
It's he's very much like, hey I thought
these could be of interest of you.

00:22:33.330 --> 00:22:34.970
Like these are my thoughts and
they replied.

00:22:34.970 --> 00:22:36.140
It's great.

00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:37.830
&gt;&gt; And that's the smartest thing to do.

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:41.460
I think that probably one of
the most valuable things that

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:42.660
we've all experienced here.

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:44.680
It's just being able to ask questions.

00:22:44.680 --> 00:22:46.670
So part of the learning process,

00:22:46.670 --> 00:22:51.840
outside the classroom is just being
immersed in, in a national perspective and

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:55.110
being immersed in learning
about Kimberly Clark in Mexico.

00:22:55.110 --> 00:22:59.904
Or planning trips or BVLs, and there's so
many different opportunities to immerse

00:22:59.904 --> 00:23:03.087
ourselves and
being able to learn more about the space.

00:23:03.087 --> 00:23:06.168
So what I would like to do
is shift a little bit into

00:23:06.168 --> 00:23:09.030
thinking about career goals after school.

00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:11.870
So we're all interested in space.

00:23:13.420 --> 00:23:17.990
The two of you have had experience over
the summer learning more about the space

00:23:17.990 --> 00:23:18.650
through internship,

00:23:18.650 --> 00:23:22.840
can you talk a little bit about
where you are going after school?

00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:25.200
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:30.344
So one option for me is to go back
into consulting but this time in

00:23:30.344 --> 00:23:35.897
the consumer and retail space, and
I know that the option is there.

00:23:35.897 --> 00:23:40.781
One other option,
which is what I'm pursuing right

00:23:40.781 --> 00:23:45.420
now is actually starting my own brand.

00:23:45.420 --> 00:23:50.270
It's like a fashion brand and
it's what has been incredible to me is

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:55.230
the amount of support that I've actually
got here from an academic point of view

00:23:55.230 --> 00:23:59.153
in terms of classes that I'm taking
that are supporting my objective.

00:23:59.153 --> 00:24:02.550
The professors, and
the knowledge that they bring, and

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:04.263
the mentoring that they do.

00:24:04.263 --> 00:24:09.307
And then once again, the alumni network,
and the willingness that they have in

00:24:09.307 --> 00:24:14.443
sharing their own learnings, especially
in the more entrepreneurship space.

00:24:14.443 --> 00:24:20.411
So those are my two options.

00:24:20.411 --> 00:24:24.829
It's like an evolving thinking process.

00:24:24.829 --> 00:24:26.475
There's time before graduation.

00:24:26.475 --> 00:24:31.798
But I can see how now, options
are starting to sort of pop up in front

00:24:31.798 --> 00:24:37.330
of you, and you almost have too many
options to choose, I would say.

00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:42.060
And so I think that was
also extremely valuable for

00:24:42.060 --> 00:24:46.378
me to see that despite the fact
that we are in this tech.

00:24:46.378 --> 00:24:53.234
[LAUGH] It's a tech-focused area, still,
there are so many opportunities for

00:24:53.234 --> 00:24:58.465
people who are interested in
the CPG consumer retail space.

00:24:58.465 --> 00:25:02.831
&gt;&gt; And I think that's one of the things to
highlight is that obviously we've all done

00:25:02.831 --> 00:25:05.813
the hard work to make sure
that those options are there.

00:25:05.813 --> 00:25:09.973
But even properly, the person that
certainly set amount of work has found

00:25:09.973 --> 00:25:14.066
that there's multiple options and
multiple career path in this space,

00:25:14.066 --> 00:25:16.703
which has been a huge opportunity for
myself.

00:25:16.703 --> 00:25:18.750
&gt;&gt; And they're all so tempting.

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:20.070
&gt;&gt; Yes [LAUGH].

00:25:20.070 --> 00:25:25.160
&gt;&gt; It's so hard to be choiceful in what
you want to do after the GSB, because

00:25:25.160 --> 00:25:29.740
you just get tempted by so many cool
things that you can do after you graduate.

00:25:29.740 --> 00:25:34.694
So I found the CMC super helpful in trying
to determine what is exactly that I

00:25:34.694 --> 00:25:38.246
want out of my next job, and
how do I stay true to that?

00:25:38.246 --> 00:25:43.500
So I've had a lot of one-on-one advising
conversations with advisors at the CMC,

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:48.310
to really write down in hard stone,
what is it exactly that I'm looking for,

00:25:48.310 --> 00:25:50.541
and what do I want out of my next job.

00:25:50.541 --> 00:25:52.041
&gt;&gt; And you know what you want.

00:25:52.041 --> 00:25:52.609
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:25:52.609 --> 00:25:54.259
&gt;&gt; So maybe you can share a little
bit with us about what you're

00:25:54.259 --> 00:25:54.985
planning to do next year.

00:25:54.985 --> 00:25:57.587
&gt;&gt; So after at least five months.

00:25:57.587 --> 00:25:59.369
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] not even next year.

00:25:59.369 --> 00:26:00.840
&gt;&gt; I know.

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:04.130
I'm going to be doing a Retail
Leadership Rotational Program at Amazon.

00:26:05.800 --> 00:26:10.145
I'll be able to test out different
career paths within a very

00:26:10.145 --> 00:26:13.715
consumer-focused industry, so I'll be
rotating between marketing operations,

00:26:13.715 --> 00:26:17.150
third-party vendors, in different areas.

00:26:17.150 --> 00:26:21.438
I like the ability to be able
to use a lot of the skills that

00:26:21.438 --> 00:26:26.660
I've learned at the GSB, so
operations are not my strength.

00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:31.411
But being able to know how to do it is so
important, just because as you move up,

00:26:31.411 --> 00:26:35.130
if you become a category leader, you'll
have operations people reporting to you.

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:37.720
So knowing what they
do is super important.

00:26:37.720 --> 00:26:42.680
So I really value having that broad
landscape of skills and experiences,

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:46.047
which this rotational program
is going to do for me.

00:26:46.047 --> 00:26:50.078
&gt;&gt; I think that's fantastic, and I know
you'll be able to use the experience, and

00:26:50.078 --> 00:26:53.873
basically, the proximity to where we
are now to build into that e-commerce

00:26:53.873 --> 00:26:56.028
platform, so I think that's fantastic.

00:26:56.028 --> 00:27:01.889
And Christina, you understated a bit
of what you've been doing as well.

00:27:01.889 --> 00:27:05.434
So Christina, I'm going to take
this opportunity to brag about her,

00:27:05.434 --> 00:27:09.724
has been working around the clock, and
has really just harnessed so much interest

00:27:09.724 --> 00:27:14.740
from our classmates, and what she's been
working on in the entrepreneurial space.

00:27:14.740 --> 00:27:19.630
And although it's entrepreneurial, it's
also very driven by her consumer interest,

00:27:19.630 --> 00:27:22.030
and I think the opportunities
that you've had and

00:27:22.030 --> 00:27:26.760
the exposure to everything in this
area has been beyond valuable.

00:27:26.760 --> 00:27:30.544
And to say that it's the community,
I think, is the wrong way to angle it.

00:27:30.544 --> 00:27:34.148
I think that you've just done
a tremendous job driving that forward and

00:27:34.148 --> 00:27:36.731
figuring out exactly where
your passions are, and

00:27:36.731 --> 00:27:39.629
[INAUDIBLE] these past years
have really created for you.

00:27:39.629 --> 00:27:45.050
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and business school is such
a risk-free environment to do so.

00:27:45.050 --> 00:27:49.010
There's so
many of us that are testing products,

00:27:49.010 --> 00:27:52.430
services, not just in the consumer
retail space, everywhere.

00:27:52.430 --> 00:27:57.755
And we work altogether at the co-lab,
which is a shared working space.

00:27:57.755 --> 00:28:03.375
Their ideas, they're throwing from corner
to the other of the room all the time,

00:28:03.375 --> 00:28:05.020
and it's risk-free.

00:28:05.020 --> 00:28:08.565
You know that you can always go out,
look out there, and

00:28:08.565 --> 00:28:13.855
find plenty of opportunities, but for
some of us, it will be a full-time career.

00:28:13.855 --> 00:28:16.391
Entrepreneurship will turn
into something full-time.

00:28:16.391 --> 00:28:17.561
&gt;&gt; Definitely.

00:28:17.561 --> 00:28:19.921
&gt;&gt; As someone that's not doing
an entrepreneurial role,

00:28:19.921 --> 00:28:21.635
being able to test out all the products.

00:28:21.635 --> 00:28:22.522
&gt;&gt; Yes [LAUGH].

00:28:22.522 --> 00:28:23.970
&gt;&gt; It's amazing [LAUGH].

00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:25.431
&gt;&gt; That is true, that is true.

00:28:25.431 --> 00:28:28.850
And I know for myself, I don't plan
to go the entrepreneurial route.

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:32.130
But I'm still trying to figure
out which space is right for me.

00:28:32.130 --> 00:28:33.690
Definitely in the consumer space.

00:28:33.690 --> 00:28:37.096
I think you just have such a competitive
advantage when you can touch and

00:28:37.096 --> 00:28:40.123
feel the products and
know exactly who you're marketing for.

00:28:40.123 --> 00:28:45.095
So ideally, I want to be a user of
the product that I eventually end up

00:28:45.095 --> 00:28:51.311
marketing, or the company I end up working
for, so still trying to figure that out.

00:28:51.311 --> 00:28:56.300
But with that said, I'd love to just
ask you guys one final question.

00:28:56.300 --> 00:29:01.220
If there's anything, any type of advice
that you would like to recommend to

00:29:01.220 --> 00:29:06.100
other students that might
potentially join the GSB community,

00:29:06.100 --> 00:29:08.814
any just lasting words or
pieces of advice for them?

00:29:08.814 --> 00:29:12.695
&gt;&gt; I think that once you get into the GSB,
you have so many opportunities,

00:29:12.695 --> 00:29:14.200
it's very overwhelming.

00:29:14.200 --> 00:29:17.150
It's hard to choose what
courses you want to take,

00:29:17.150 --> 00:29:19.290
what extracurriculars you
want to be involved with.

00:29:20.850 --> 00:29:22.930
My biggest advice is dream big.

00:29:22.930 --> 00:29:26.600
Whenever you're trying to make choices in
terms of what skills do I want to build,

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:27.800
what classes do I want to take,

00:29:27.800 --> 00:29:31.240
think about the wildest role
that you think you might have.

00:29:31.240 --> 00:29:35.170
Like, I'm going to be CEO of X, and
what do I need to get there, and

00:29:35.170 --> 00:29:40.510
then leverage the GSB to do as much
as you can to prepare yourself for

00:29:40.510 --> 00:29:43.900
that huge big role that
you're going to have one day.

00:29:43.900 --> 00:29:44.915
&gt;&gt; I think that's so true.

00:29:44.915 --> 00:29:46.716
For me, it's be open-minded.

00:29:46.716 --> 00:29:51.675
If I had even guessed the number of things
that I'd be involved in two years ago,

00:29:51.675 --> 00:29:54.080
I think I would have been so far-off.

00:29:54.080 --> 00:29:57.970
I mean, I had the opportunity to write
a case on the mental health space,

00:29:57.970 --> 00:30:01.310
and I would have never said that
I would do that two years ago.

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:05.030
And then I've been able intern in
a couple of really incredible companies,

00:30:05.030 --> 00:30:07.290
over this summer,
focused on the consumer space.

00:30:07.290 --> 00:30:12.160
And the access that we get to a number
of leaders is just unbelievable,

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:14.380
so I'd just say be open-minded.

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:15.460
Don't limit yourself.

00:30:15.460 --> 00:30:16.930
Don't come in with hard and

00:30:16.930 --> 00:30:20.988
fast rules about what you have to do,
and won't do, for that matter.

00:30:20.988 --> 00:30:25.030
But be open-minded, set a plan for
what you really want to accomplish, and

00:30:25.030 --> 00:30:26.010
just go after it.

00:30:26.010 --> 00:30:30.474
So I think that I've learned the most from
those unexpected experiences, and I think

00:30:30.474 --> 00:30:34.945
that's the best advice that I can give to
anyone that's thinking about coming here.

00:30:34.945 --> 00:30:39.658
So with that, I'd like to thank both
of you for being here today, and

00:30:39.658 --> 00:30:42.686
we wish you all a wonderful
rest of your day.

00:30:42.686 --> 00:30:44.080
Thank you.

00:30:44.080 --> 00:30:53.320
[MUSIC]

